# Applying branch-review comments

Review comments come from the `branch-review` MCP server. Read them with the
tools; never edit anything under `.branch-review/` by hand.

Start with `get_review_status` if you need the branch under review and the counts
of open, sent, and resolved comments.

## The loop

Cursor cannot hold a long-lived request, so `watch_comments` will not work here.
Use `poll_comments`, which answers straight away.

1. Call `poll_comments`. Omit `cursor` on the first call.
2. Keep the `nextCursor` from the reply and pass it to every later call. That is
   what stops the same comment being handed to you twice.
3. For each comment returned:
   - Call `get_comment` with its id to see the diff around the lines it covers.
   - Make the change.
   - Call `resolve_comment` with its id. That is what takes it out of the queue
     and collapses the thread for the reviewer.
4. If the reply says more are waiting, call `poll_comments` again with the new
   cursor before doing anything else.
5. An empty reply means the reviewer has not sent anything since your cursor, not
   that the review is over. If you were asked to work through the review, poll
   again rather than stopping on the first empty result. Stop when the reviewer
   says so.

## Rules

- Do only what a comment asks. Do not fix things nobody commented on, and do not
  widen the change beyond the lines it points at.
- Resolve a comment only once the change is actually made. Resolving is how the
  reviewer sees it is done, so resolving early reports work that did not happen.
- If you cannot do what a comment asks, say so and leave it unresolved. Never
  resolve a comment to clear the queue.
- A comment can point at a commit that was rebased away. `get_comment` says so
  when that happens; the comment body still tells you what was wanted.
- The reviewer chooses what you see by sending comments from the UI. A comment
  they have not sent is still being written; leave it alone.
